A1. Here are some splendid openings for ambitious young men who don't mind nightwork. ~ SAFES
A6. The Elks at college (pl.). ~ FRATS
A11. Baby smear. ~ FARINA
A12. Best smellers. ~ LILACS
A14. Fossil museum at Washington. ~ CAPITOL
A15. To come again and again. ~ ITERATE
A17. Rug preservers. ~ ASHES
A18. To put the O. K. to. ~ AGREE
A20. What a nautical minded Frenchman puts out to. ~ MER
A21. Some early American painters. ~ UTES
A22. What the horse carrying your bet usually does. ~ TROTS
A23. This was thrown over a river. ~ SPAN
A24. Where to go for a roll before breakfast. ~ SEA
A25. Holiday weather (pl.). ~ RAINS
A26. Moonlight hooey. ~ POEMS
A27. Got in on the ground floor with (such English, my!). ~ ENDEARED
A29. Time does this to milk and marriage. ~ SOURS
A30. They call it this after the stock is watered (pl.). ~ CONSOMMES
A32. How the Latins would say Lindbergh flew the Big Drink. ~ SOLUS
A34. Just one of the unemployed. ~ LOITERER
A38. This one is simply slaying. ~ FATAL
A39. Two pairs of fives. ~ FISTS
A40. Hebrew mammy. ~ EVE
A41. Belles lettres (abbr.). ~ LITT
A42. Not quite sophomoronic at college. ~ FRESH
A43. Pressed the little dinkus with the foot. ~ SPED
A44. The best part of polo. ~ OLO
A45. Publisher's fairy tale. ~ BLURB
A46. The kind of a romance that cools off quickest. ~ FIERY
A47. A hot city that had a hot ending. ~ POMPEII
A49. Alive. ~ ANIMATE
A51. Went on a tear again. ~ RERENT
A52. Makes happy noises with the throat. ~ CAROLS
A53. Higher word of criticism. ~ NERTS
A54. Bemoans in the Irish manner. ~ KEENS

D1. This is ivory-tipped. ~ SAPHEAD
D2. You'll find this over your head. ~ ARIES
D3. These are seldom found in ready-made clothes. ~ FITS
D4. His salts are duty-free in India. ~ ENO
D5. These do the disappearing act every Saturday. ~ SALARIES
D6. Marriage vow breakers. ~ FLIRTS
D7. These are put on at church every Sunday. ~ RITES
D8. On the safe side. ~ ALEE
D9. Soft feather bed. ~ TAR
D10. Puppy fun. ~ SCAMPER
D11. This is what they do when they tie up in Wall Street. ~ FASTEN
D13. Hot stuff in a Turkish bath (pl.). ~ STEAMS
D14. Reason. ~ CAUSE
D16. Oceanic air travelers. ~ ERNS
D19. This was bawled out by a Venetian traffic cop. ~ GONDOLIER
D22. Scotch lakes. ~ TARNS
D23. A sufferer from chronic hangovers. ~ SOUSE
D25. This might be a fast French male. ~ RAOUL
D26. These can't make trees. ~ POETS
D28. Highbrow shine. ~ ECLAT
D29. The man in the brown derby. ~ SMITH
D31. Something a rolling stone will never become. ~ MOSSBACK
D32. Fleet boy. ~ SAILOR
D33. The kind of men women put their feet all over. ~ OTTOMEN
D35. What Congress rarely does. ~ REPEALS
D36. What the wary young man does to trouble. ~ EVERTS
D37. What girls often do when they change color. ~ REDYE
D38. A suite on the Bowery. ~ FLOP
D39. These hold tree-hanging endurance records for all time. ~ FRUITS
D42. Sparker. ~ FLINT
D43. This is awfully simple. ~ SIMON
D45. Drought relief. ~ BEER
D46. Janitors never do this till they see the whites on your windows. ~ FIRE
D48. This comes before war liquor. ~ PRE
D50. Well, here it is again:—"What Scotch girl said when she meant 'Yes!'." ~ NAE


